#import "../prelude.typ": *

#definition(title: "Brauer group")[
  Let $k$ be a field and $k_s$ be one of its separable closures. The #term[Brauer group] of $k$ is
  $
    "Br"(k) := H^2(k,k_s)
  $
  If $K|k$ is a separable subextension of $k_s|k$ we can define the #term[relative Brauer group] of the extension as
  $
    "Br"(K|k) := ker("Br"(k) -->^"Res" "Br"(K))
  $
]

The elements of $"Br"(k)$ correspond to the finite dimensional division algebras $D$ with center $k$. In this correspondence, the subgroup $"Br"(K|k)$ gives those $D$ for which $D times.o_k K tilde.eq M_(n times n)(K)$, where $n^2 = dim_k D$.

#lemma[
  1. If $K|k$ is a Galois extension with group $G$ then it holds $"Br"(K|k)tilde.eq H^2(G,K^times)$;
  2. The Brauer group $"Br"(k)$ is the union of all the subgroups $"Br"(K|k)$ for finite Galois extensions $K|k$;
  3. If $G$ is finite and cyclic it holds $"Br"(K|k) tilde.eq k^times / (N_(K|k)(K^times))$.
]<lemma:brauer>

Here $N_(K|k) : K^times -> k^times$ denotes the norm of the field extension. When $K|k$ is finite and Galois it has value
$
  N_(K|k)(a)=product_(sigma in "Gal"(K|k)) sigma a
$
for every $a in K^times$.

#proof[
  1. From #link(<missing-ref>)[Hilbert's theorem 90] we have $H^1(G,K^times)=0$. As seen in #link(<missing-ref>)[\#] this makes the following inflation-restriction sequence exact: $
    0 --> H^2(G,K^times) -->^"Inf" H^2(k,k_s) -->^"Res" H^2(K,k_s),
  $ and by definition $"Br"(K|k)= ker "Res"=H^2(G,K^times)$.
  2. From the previous point we deduce $
    "Br"(k) = lim_--> H^2(G,K^times) =^((1)) lim_--> "Br"(K|k)= union.big "Br"(K|k).
    $
  3. As seen in #link(<missing-ref>)[\#] when the Galois group $G$ is cyclic we have $
    "Br"(K|k) =^((1)) H^2(G,K^times)=(K^times)^G / (N_(K|k)(K^times)) = k^times / (N_(K|k)(K^times)).
    $
]

#example[
  0. When the field $k$ is complete we have $"Br"(k) = 0$ since the Galois action on it is trivial.
  1. Let's compute the Brauer group of the real numbers: $
    "Br"(RR) = "Br"(CC|RR) = H^2("Gal"(CC|RR),CC^times) = RR^times / (N_(CC|RR)(CC^times)) = RR^times / RR^times_(>0) tilde.eq ZZ\/2ZZ.
  $
  2. When $k = FF_q$ is a finite field the Brauer group vanishes: $"Br"(FF_q) = 0$. By the #link(<lemma:brauer>)[lemma 2)] it is enough to show that $"Br"(FF_(q^r)|FF_q) = 0$ for every $r > 0$. Recall the following facts:
    - The group $"Gal"(FF_(q^r)|FF_q)$ is cyclic, generated by the frobenius $a |-> a^q$. In particular the norm has value $
      N(a)=a^((q^r-1)/(q-1));
    $
    - The multiplicative group $FF_(q^r)^times$ is cyclic.
    Let $omega$ be a generator of $FF_(q^r)^times$, its norm $N(omega)$ by the above calculation has order $q-1$ and therefore generates $FF_q$. This shows $N$ is surjective and by #link(<lemma:brauer>)[lemma 3)] we deduce $"Br"(FF_(q^r)|FF_q) = 0$.
]

== The path to $K|QQ_p$

The main goal of this section is proving that if $K|QQ_p$ is a $p$-adic number field then its Brauer group is $"Br"(K) = QQ \/ ZZ$.

